insert_before("sections/05-rq-evidence.tex", "\\subsection{RQ2: diffusion against autoregressive models}",
              r"\paragraph{Continuous and flow-based text models.} Two recent continuous models report likelihood and generation quality competitive with discrete diffusion: LangFlow, which links embedding-space diffusion to flow matching~\cite{arXiv:2604.11748}, and CoDAR, which keeps diffusion continuous and rounds to tokens with a contextual autoregressive decoder~\cite{arXiv:2603.02547}. Theory also narrows the gap in parallelism: uniform and Gaussian diffusion can sample in a number of forward passes that scales with the dual total correlation of the data, which was previously known only for masked diffusion~\cite{arXiv:2609.20539}. In the notation these are changes of channel (C1) and, for CoDAR, of the final rounding step, not new commitment policies."
              "\n\n")

insert_before("sections/05-rq-evidence.tex", r"\textbf{Verdict: mixed} (22 signed studies;",
              r"\paragraph{Compute-matched studies.} Studies designed to make this comparison at matched compute sharpen the picture without reversing the verdict. With single-pass training, masked diffusion scales at a rate comparable to autoregressive models with a relatively small compute gap~\cite{arXiv:2410.18514}. A FLOP-matched study of masked, uniform-state and interpolating diffusion finds that a cross-entropy objective makes masked diffusion about 12\% more FLOP-efficient, that perplexity is informative within a diffusion family but can mislead across families, and that a family with worse likelihood scaling can sit better on the speed--quality Pareto frontier because it samples faster~\cite{arXiv:2602.15014}. When unique data rather than compute is the constraint, the order reverses: with repeated epochs diffusion overtakes autoregressive training beyond a critical compute~\cite{arXiv:2507.15857,arXiv:2511.03276}. These studies say when a diffusion model is the better choice (data-constrained pretraining, or deployment in which parallel decoding speed at a fixed quality matters), which the best-against-best comparisons of the sample cannot. They also support reading the mixed verdict as a statement about how the sampled studies compare, at unmatched budgets, rather than about the model families."
              "\n")

insert_before("sections/05-rq-evidence.tex", r"\textbf{Verdict: mixed} (15 sweeping studies;",
              r"\paragraph{Sampler-centric studies.} Recent work outside the sample explains why curves bend and offers levers. An oracle study that replaces the learned denoiser with the exact posterior of a hidden Markov model shows that few-step samplers are not distributionally correct even with a perfect denoiser, with transition-level mismatch that vanishes only as the number of steps approaches the sequence length; better NLL, generative perplexity or MAUVE does not imply correct sampling~\cite{arXiv:2602.19619}. Dependency-aware selection (DEMASK) predicts pairwise dependence between masked positions and co-commits only positions with bounded cumulative dependence, which bounds the total-variation gap to the model's joint under a sub-additivity assumption, with a reported 1.7 to 2.2 times speed-up at matched or better accuracy~\cite{arXiv:2604.02560}. A learned commitment controller (TraceLock) replaces hand-set confidence rules with a policy trained on the future stability of decoding traces and transfers across window widths, lengths and step budgets~\cite{arXiv:2605.24697}. Both act on the position rule of C4, not on the network. For revision (C5), a re-evaluation finds that a post-hoc confidence-based remasking method brings little or no benefit over confidence-based unmasking at the usual short block lengths, and partly offsets the errors added by more stochastic decoding~\cite{arXiv:2606.12232}; a remasking loop is a remedy whose value depends on stochasticity and block size, not a default."
              "\n")

insert_before("sections/05-rq-evidence.tex", r"\textbf{Verdict: mixed} (five signed studies",
              r"\paragraph{Fully continuous unified models.} A recent unified model, Multimodal Flow, organises text blocks and images as ordered continuous chunks under one chunk-causal flow backbone that learns a single vector field~\cite{arXiv:2609.40362}. Because both modalities then share one objective and one sampler, joint and decoupled arms built on such a model would differ only in what they share, which is the comparison a synergy test needs."
              "\n")

